\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 4: Single-Equation Linear Model and OLS Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Omitted Variable Problem and Measurement Error
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
4.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Ordinary Least Squares Solutions to the Omitted Variables Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
4.3.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Ordinary Least Squares Ignoring the Omitted Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because it is so prevalent in applied work, we now consider the omitted
 varaibles problem in more detail.
 A model that assumes an additive effect of the omitted variable is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y|x_{1},\ldots,x_{K},q)=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{K}+\gamma q
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is the omitted factor.
 In particular, we are interested in the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

, which are the partial effects of the observed explanatory variables, holding
 the other explanatory variables constant, 
\emph on
including
\emph default
 the unobservable 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

.
 In the context of this additive mdoel, there is no point in allowing for
 more than one unobservable; any omitted factors are lumped into 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

.
 Henceforth, we simply refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 as the omitted variable.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Viewing equation (1) as a structural model, we can always write it in error
 form as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y & = & \beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{K}+\gamma q+v\\
E(v|x_{1},\ldots,x_{K},q) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is the 
\series bold
structural error
\series default
.
 One way to handle the nonobservability of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is to put it into the error term.
 In doing so, nothing is lost by assuming 
\begin_inset Formula $E(q)=0$
\end_inset

, because an intercept is included in equation (2).
 Putting 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 into the rror terms means we rewrite equation (2) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y & = & \beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{K}+u\\
u & \equiv & \gamma q+v
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

The error in equation (4) consists of two parts.
 Under equation (3), 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 has zero mean and is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},\ldots,x_{K}$
\end_inset

 (and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

).
 By normalization, 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 also has zero mean.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u)=0$
\end_inset

.
 However, 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is correlated with any of the regressors, then so is 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, and we have an endogeneity problem.
 We cannot expect OLS to consistently estimate 
\emph on
any
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Although 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u|x)\neq E(u)$
\end_inset

 in equation (4), the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 do have a structural interpretation because they appear in equation (2).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is easy to characterize the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim s}$
\end_inset

 of the OLS estimators when the omitted variable is ignored; we will call
 this the 
\series bold
OLS omitted variables inconsistency
\series default
 or 
\series bold
OLS omitted variables bias
\series default
 (even though the latter term is not always precise).
 Write the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 onto the observable explanatory variables as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\delta_{K}x_{K}+r
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where, by definition of a linear projection, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(r)=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(x_{j},r)=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,K$
\end_inset

.
 The parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{j}$
\end_inset

 measures the relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

 after 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

partialing out
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the other 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{h}$
\end_inset

.
 Then we can easily infer the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}$
\end_inset

 of the OLS estimators from regressing 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $1,x_{1},\ldots,x_{K}$
\end_inset

 by finding an equation that does satisfy Assumptions OLS.1 and OLS.2.
 Plugging in equation (6) into equation (2) and doing simple algebra gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=(\beta_{0}+\gamma\delta_{0})+(\beta_{1}+\gamma\delta_{1})x_{1}+\cdots+(\beta_{K}+\gamma\delta_{K})x_{K}+v+\gamma r
\]

\end_inset

Now, the error 
\begin_inset Formula $v+\gamma r$
\end_inset

 has zero mean and is uncorrelated with each regressor.
 It follows that we can just read off the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}$
\end_inset

 of the OLS estimators from the regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $1,x_{1},\ldots,x_{K}$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}\ \hat{\beta}_{j}=\beta_{j}+\gamma\delta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Sometimes it is assumed that most of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{j}$
\end_inset

 are zero.
 When the correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and a particular variable, say 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

, is the focus, a common (usually implicit) assumption is that all 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{j}$
\end_inset

 in equation (6) except the intercept and coefficient on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 are zero.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{j}=\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,K-1$
\end_inset

, and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{p\lim}\ \hat{\beta}_{K}=\beta_{K}+\gamma\left[\frac{\mbox{Cov}(x_{K},q)}{\mbox{Var}(x_{K})}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

(because 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{K}=\nicefrac{\mbox{Cov}(x_{K},q)}{\mbox{Var}(x_{K})}$
\end_inset

 in this case).
 This formula gives us a simple way to determine the sign, and perhaps the
 magnitude, of the inconsistency in 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 are positively correlated, the asymptotic bias is positive.
 The other combinations are easily worked out.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 has substantial variation in the population relative to the covariance
 between 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

, then the bias can be small.
 In the general case of equation (6), it is difficult to sign 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{K}$
\end_inset

 because it measures a partial correlation.
 It is for this reason that 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{j}=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,K-1$
\end_inset

 is often maintained for determining the likely asymptotic bias in 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{K}$
\end_inset

 when only 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is endogenous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
4.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Properties of Ordinary Least Squares under Measurement Error
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another way that endogenous explanatory variables can arise in economic
 applications occurs when one or more of the variables in our model contains
 
\series bold
measurement error
\series default
.
 In this section, we derive the consequences of measurement error for ordinary
 least squares estimation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The measurement error problem has a statistical structure similar to the
 omitted variable problem discussed in the previous section.
 However, they are conceptually very different.
 In the measurement error case, the variable that we do not observe has
 a well-defined, quantitative meaning, but our measures of it may contain
 error.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another important difference between the omitted variable and measurment
 error problems is that, in the latter case, often the mismeasured explanatory
 variable is the one whose effect is of primary interest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Before we turn to the analysis, it is important to remember that measurement
 error is an issue only when the variables on which we can collect data
 differ from the variables that influence decisions by individuals, families,
 firms, and so on.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
4.4.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Measurement Error in the Dependent Variable
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We begin with the case where the dependent variable is the only variable
 measured with error.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

 denote the variable (in the population, as always) that we would like to
 explain.
 The regression model has the usual linear form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y^{*}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{K}+v
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and we assume that is satisfies at least Assumptions OLS.1 and OLS.2.
 Typically, we are interested in 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y^{*}|x_{1},\ldots,x_{K})$
\end_inset

.
 We let 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 represent the observable measure of 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $y\neq y^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The population measurement error is defined as the difference between the
 observed value and the actual value:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
e_{0}=y-y^{*}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For a random draw 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from the population, we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i0}=y_{i}-y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, but what is important is how the measurement error in the population is
 related to other factors.
 To obtain an estimable model, we write 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}=y-e_{0}$
\end_inset

, plug this into equation (8), and rearrange:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{K}+v+e_{0}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y,x_{1},\ldots,x_{K}$
\end_inset

 are observed, we can estimate this model by OLS.
 In effect, we just ignore the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is an imperfect measure of 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

 and proceed as usual.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When does OLS with 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 in place of 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

 produce consistent estimators of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

? Since the originial model (8) satisfies Assumption OLS.1, 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 has zero mean and its uncorrelated with each 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 It is only natural to assume that the measurement error has zero mean;
 if it does not, this fact only affects estimation of the intercept, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Much more important is what we assume about the relationship between measuremen
t error 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{0}$
\end_inset

 and the explanatory variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 The usual assumption is that the measurement error in 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is statistically independent of each explanatory variable, which implies
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{0}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the OLS estimators from equation (10) are consistent (and possibly
 unbiased as well).
 Furhter, the usual OLS inference procedures (
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistics, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 statistics, 
\begin_inset Formula $LM$
\end_inset

 statistics) are asymptotically valid under appropriate homoskedasticity
 assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated, as is usually assumed, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(v+e_{0})=\sigma_{v}^{2}+\sigma_{0}^{2}>\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the measurement error in the dependent variable results in a
 larger error variance than wehn the dependent variable is not measured
 with error.
 This result is hardly surprising and translates into larger asymptotic
 variances for the OLS estimators than if we could observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 But the larger error variance violates none of the assumptions needed for
 OLS estimation to have its desirable large-sample properties.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
4.4.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Measurement Error in an Explanatory Variable
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Traditionally, measurement error in an explanatory variable has been considered
 a much more important problem than measurement error in the response variable.
 In this subsection, we develop the general case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We consider the model with a single explanatory measured with error
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{K}^{*}+v
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y,x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1}$
\end_inset

 are observable but 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is not.
 We assume at a minimum that 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 has zero mean is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{K-1},x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

; in fact, we usually have in mind the structural model 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1},x_{K}^{*})=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 were observed, OLS estimation would produce consistent estimators.
 Instead, we have a measure of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

; call it 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 A maintained assumption is that 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is also uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 This follows under the redundancy assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y|x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1},x_{K}^{*},x_{K})=E(y|x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1},x_{K}^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 This means that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 has no effect on 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 once the other explanatory variables, including 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

, have been controlled for.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is assumed to be the variable that affects 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, this assumption is uncontroversial.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The measurement error in the population is simply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
e_{K}=x_{K}-x_{K}^{*}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and this can be positive, negative, or zero.
 We assume that the average measurement error in the population is zero:
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(e_{K})=0$
\end_inset

, which has no practical consequences because we include an intercept in
 equation (11).
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is assumed to be uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is also uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We want to know the properties of OLS if we simply replace 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 and run the regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $1,x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 These depend crucially on the assumptions we make about the measurement
 error.
 An assumption that is almost always maintained is that 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with the explanatory variables not measured with error:
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{j}e_{K})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,K-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The key assumptions involve the relationship between the measurement error
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 Two assumptions have been the focus in econometrics literature, and these
 represent polar extremes.
 The first assumption is that 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with the 
\emph on
observed
\series bold
 
\series default
\emph default
measure, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Cov}(x_{K},e_{K})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

From equation (12), if assumption (13) is true, then 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

 must be correlated with the unobserved 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 To determine the properties of OLS in this case, we write 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}=x_{K}-e_{K}$
\end_inset

 and plug this into equation (11):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{K}+(v-\beta_{K}e_{K})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Now, we have assumed that 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

 both have zero mean and are uncorrelated with each 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

, including 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

; therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $v-\beta_{K}e_{K}$
\end_inset

 has zero mean and is uncorrelated with the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 It follows that the OLS estimation with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 in place of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 produces consistent estimators of all the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 (assuming the standard rank condition Assumption OLS.2).
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

, the variance of the error in equation (14) is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(v-\beta_{K}e_{K})=\sigma_{v}^{2}+\beta_{K}^{2}\sigma_{e_{K}}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, except when 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{K}=0$
\end_inset

, measurement error increases the error variance, which is not a surprising
 finding and violates note of the OLS assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 implies that OLS has all its nice properties, this is not usually what
 econometricians have in mind when referring to measurement error in the
 explanatory variable.
 The 
\series bold
classical errors-in-variables (CEV)
\series default
 assumption replaces assumption (13) with the assumption that the measurement
 error is uncorrelated with the 
\emph on
unobserved 
\emph default
explanatory variable:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Cov}(x_{K}^{*},e_{K})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This assumption comes from writing the observed measure as the sum of the
 true explanatory variable and the measurement error, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}=x_{K}^{*}+e_{K}$
\end_inset

, and then assuming the two components of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated.
 (This has nothing to do with assumptions about 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

; we are always maintaining that 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

, and therefore with 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If assumption (15) holds, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
must
\emph default
 be correlated:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Cov}(x_{K},e_{K})=E(x_{K}e_{K})=E(x_{K}^{*}e_{K})+E(e_{K}^{2})=\sigma_{e_{K}}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus, under the CEV assumption, the covariance between 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

 is equal to the variance of the measurement error.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Looking at equation (14), we see that correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{K}$
\end_inset

 causes problems for OLS.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated, the covariance between 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 and the composite error 
\begin_inset Formula $v-\beta_{K}e_{K}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(x_{K},v-\beta_{K}e_{K})=-\beta_{K}\mbox{Cov}(x_{K},e_{K})=-\beta_{K}\sigma_{e_{K}}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 It follows that, in the CEV case, the OLS regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},\ldots,x_{K}$
\end_inset

 generally gives inconsistent estimators of 
\emph on
all
\emph default
 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim s}$
\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $j\neq k$
\end_inset

 are difficult to characterize except under special assumptions.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

, all 
\begin_inset Formula $j\neq K$
\end_inset

, then so is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

, and it follows that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{plim}\ \hat{\beta}_{j}=\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

, all 
\begin_inset Formula $j\neq K$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{K}$
\end_inset

 can be characterized in any case.
 We can show that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{p\lim}(\hat{\beta}_{K})=\beta_{K}\left(\frac{\sigma_{r_{K}^{*}}^{2}}{\sigma_{r_{K}^{*}}^{2}+\sigma_{e_{K}}^{2}}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the linear projection error in
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{K}^{*}=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\delta_{K-1}x_{K-1}+r_{K}^{*}
\]

\end_inset

An important implication of equation (17) is that, because the term multiplying
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{K}$
\end_inset

 is always between zero and one, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mbox{p\lim}(\hat{\beta}_{K})\right|<\left|\beta_{K}\right|$
\end_inset

.
 This is called the 
\series bold
attenuation bias
\series default
 in OLS due to CEVs: on average (or in large samples), the estimated OLS
 effect will be 
\emph on
attenuated
\emph default
 as a result of the presence of CEVs.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{K}$
\end_inset

 is positive, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{K}$
\end_inset

 will tend to underestimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{K}$
\end_inset

; if 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{K}$
\end_inset

 is negative, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{K}$
\end_inset

 will tend to overestimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{K}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the case of a single explanatory variable measured with error, equation
 (17) becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{p\lim}(\hat{\beta}_{1})=\beta_{1}\left(\frac{\sigma_{x_{1}^{*}}^{2}}{\sigma_{x_{1}^{*}}^{2}+\sigma_{e_{1}}^{2}}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The term multiplying 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 in equation (18) is 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\mbox{Var}(x_{1}^{*})}{\mbox{Var}(x_{1})}$
\end_inset

, which is always less than unity under the CEV assumption (15).
 As 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(e_{1})$
\end_inset

 shrinks relative to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(x_{1}^{*})$
\end_inset

, the attenuation bias disappears.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the case with multiple explanatory variables, equation (17) shows that
 it is not 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x_{K}^{*}}^{2}$
\end_inset

 that affect 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}(\hat{\beta}_{K})$
\end_inset

 but the variance in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 after netting out the other explanatory variables.
 Thus, the more collinear 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is with other explanatory variables, the worse is the attenuation bias.
\end_layout

